The primary function of  the turbine protection system is to detect turbine disturbances at an early stage and initiate appropriate safe shutdown operations to limit damage, repair costs and downtime for generator sets. It is active all the time and cannot depend on operator intervention. Shutdown protection is triggered by the following violations (the list of  protections is not limited to this list):
overspeed;
excess pressure in the turbine condenser;
overpressure, last stage;
high level of  condensate, last stage;
temperature rise of bearings (plain bearings and thrust bearings);
low lubricating oil pressure;
excess temperature of the lubricating oil;
low lube oil level;
vibrations (turbine shaft or bearing frame);
axial displacement of the shaft;
fire protection (fire protection button)+ external fire protection;
shutdown of the turbine through the shutdown button and external shutdown;
overpressure, main supply steam and hot superheat;
increased / decreased frequency of the rotor;
excess seal steam pressure;
steam temperature excess on seals;
shaft or housing vibrations;
temperature rise of bearings;
cooling water leak, generator - air cooler;
temperature rise of the generator stator winding;
vibration of the generator shaft or housing;
temperature rise of generator bearings.

Input/output modules are installed in the local TCS cabinet or in the input/output cabinet of the TCS and are connected to the TCS via data transfer  protocols  (The  communication  protocol will be determined in accordance with the decision made).
All  decisions made  during  the  design process must  be consistent  with  the objectives  of the project. If it is impossible to use the regulatory documentation in accordance with the requirements of the legislation of  the Republic of  Kazakhstan, develop special technical conditions within the framework of  this project.
The  interface  for  generator  protection,  synchronization,  ARI  and  turbine  sub-distribution  is implemented  via  a  data  communication  protocol.  The  interface  provides  full-scale  operation  and visualization of  the generator.
The  signal  exchange  between  the  low  voltage  secondary  distribution  system  (  control  of the energy consumer by the steam turbine generator) and the TCS is carried out using remote inputs/outputs and  a  data  communication  protocol  connection.  Depending  on  the  scope  of  supply,  the  remote input/output modules are installed in the secondary direct current distribution system or in the central cabinet of  the TCS.
The connection between the TCS and the APCS is implemented using the OPC protocol (linking and embedding objects for process control) via the Ethernet bus connection of  the TCP/IP protocol. Data transfer is carried out in accordance with the principle server - client. Security software is included in the TCS to protect data. In addition, a separate hardware firewall is included to provide authentication and secure communication over the network.
The physical connections between the OPC server, hardware firewall and DCS are implemented as an Ethernet  connection  over  TCP/IP  protocol  as  part  of a  copper  bus  channel.  Alternatively,  the  OPC connection can be implemented as a backup. This connection includes the following:
- transmission of  measured values for the control of  the steam turbine generator;
- operation of  the turbine (controller selection and setpoint adjustment) during normal operation;
- determination of the volume of the signal;
- interface definition (physical, protocol).

