The Contractor will prescribe all the necessary procedures for both individual and complex tests. The test procedures detail the sequence of  steps that will be followed to complete the commissioning of each system, partial system and subsystem, individual item. Test procedures will describe in detail and clearly the procedure for carrying out certain stages in which increased attention must be paid.  The procedure will include, but not be limited to:
- preparatory work and minimum requirements for the start of  the test;
- detailed description of  works;
- precise criteria for successful completion of  the test;
- applicable safety measures;
- forms of  the act of  acceptance sheet, or test report;
- safety data sheets of  chemical products (if  necessary);
- a list of  equipment subject to mechanical or electrical isolation;
- a list of  specific tools and techniques.
It is possible to apply standard procedures when testing on similar equipment and if  this testing is routine.
The procedure will comply with all requirements of  the equipment manufacturer.

Completion of  pre-commissioning works in relation to facilities is considered to be achieved if:
- the  work has been completed in accordance with the Contract,  including the completion of preparations  for  pre-commissioning  works  and  the  completion  of pre-commissioning  works  in accordance with the project of  pre-commissioning works, with the exception of  deficiencies in Items of the Defect list of  D category, which are available in the List of  deficiencies and Operational tests;
- the facilities comply with applicable laws and all relevant permits;
- the Contractor has fully fulfilled its training obligations in accordance with the terms of  the Contract;
- the facilities are ready for operation;
- all deficiencies have been eliminated on the Items of  the Defect list of  A Category, on the Items of  the Defect list ofB Category and on the Items of  the Defect list of  C Category;
- -the  Contractor  provided  the  Customer  with  complete  and  final  maintenance  and  operation manuals, technical passports for equipment and construction documentation required in accordance with the Contract;
- the Contractor delivered spare parts to the construction site in accordance with the Contract;
- -the  entire  previously  agreed  package  of documentation  was  transferred.  Contents  of the functional node dossier.
The pre-commissioning works are a verification process used to confirm that the facilities are designed,  supplied,  manufactured,  installed,  tested  and  ready  for  operation  in  accordance  with  the drawings and specifications stipulated by the Contract.
In order to obtain permission to conduct pre-commissioning works, the Contractor will develop a  procedure  for  conducting  pre-commissioning  works,  which  consists  of the  following  standard activities, but is not limited to:
- development of  a system for pre-commissioning works;
- development of  a breakdown system (system matrix);
- definition of  pre-commissioning works packages;
- schedule of  pre-commissioning works;
- budget for pre-commissioning works, including spare parts for pre-commissioning;
- monitoring the readiness of  pre-commissioning works.
The pre-commissioning works procedure will include:

1. Tasks. Providing detailed information on the operational parameters to be achieved;
2. Description.  A  brief description  of the  systems/subsystems  to  be  commissioned  will  be provided;
3. Lists of  temporary equipment and consumables. A list of  all consumables, tools, temporary equipment, and vendor assistance requirements;
4. Health, Environment and Safety. A list of  all toxic and polluting liquids and materials will be compiled,  describing methods  for  their handling  and  disposal.  A  checklist  of all  necessary  security measures will be drawn up, including work permit requirements. Prior to the connection of  electrical equipment, a notice of  connection of  the power supply is issued in order to inform all parties involved about the upcoming supply of  electricity;
5. Conservation. Requirements will be developed for the removal of existing conservation/protection and subsequent new conservation of  the system if  the system is out of  service for a certain period oftime;
6. Scope. The scope of  work will contain a detailed description of  the work to be carried out step by step, including pre-commissioning works, audit trails. Faults and/or errors will be logged. Contractor commissioning procedure will be included.  Performance parameters will be included for comparison with the actual result achieved during pre-commissioning works;
7. Planning. Detailed pre-commissioning works plans for systems/subsystems will be developed;
8. Transfer. The transfer of  works will be carried out by signing an act or other documents agreed in the commissioning procedure developed by the Contractor.
The  standard  list  of activities  for  the  Completion  of pre-commissioning  works  shall  be determined  jointly with the Customer, after approval of  the design and estimate documentation.
If the Contractor  considers  that the  requirements  for  the  Completion  of pre-Commissioning works set forth in this Section are satisfied, the Contractor will notify the Customer in writing and request the Customer to issue a Certificate of  completion of  pre-commissioning works.
Within 14 days after receiving a request from the Contractor, the Customer undertakes:
- issue to the Contractor the Certificate of  completion of  pre-commissioning works, indicating the date on which the facilities reached the Completion of  pre-commissioning works in accordance with the Contract;
- reject the request, indicating the reasons and indicating the work that must be performed by the Contractor in order to receive the Certificate of  completion of  pre-commissioning works. Subject to its obligations to perform such work, the Contractor shall complete the work before receiving new notice in accordance with this section.

After the completion of  the Pre-commissioning works for all Works and the successful launch of the  facilities,  and  when  the  facilities  reach  operational  parameters,  the  Contractor  will  notify  the Customer of  the readiness of  the facilities for Operational tests, during which the facilities will operate uninterruptedly for 24 (twenty-four) hours.
The Operational tests include, but are not limited to:
- operation of facilities in various conditions to determine the operating range of  objects;
- measuring the performance of facilities, including the measurement of the composition of the original  product and  the  trend of pressure,  temperature  and product  flows  for their  throughput,  and quality;
- -comparison  of  actual  performance  with  predicted  performance  and/or  with  specifications according  to  the  projects  approved  by  the  Customer,  and  the  requirements  of the  Customer,  and confirmation of achievement of  target indicators;
- reporting results, identifying corrective actions and responsible party.
In the event that the facilities or any part of  them do not pass the Operational tests due to identified defects, the Contractor will eliminate such defects in accordance with the terms of  the Contract, and after the expiration of the  period for the elimination of defects,  a 24-hour  Operational test of the items is repeated.
The  Operational  tests,  which  are  part  of  the  Post-construction  tests,  will  be  conducted  in accordance with the conditions set forth in this Section and the terms of  the Contract.
The Contractor shall be responsible for providing all equipment, assistance, documents and other information, temporary supply of  electricity and �ater, equipment, fuel, supplies, tools, labor, materials and suitably qualified, experienced and competent personnel, as necessary for the effective and proper carrying  out these tests.  All instruments,  equipment and tools must be calibrated in accordance with applicable  local  and  international  codes  and  standards.  The  Contractor  shall provide test  equipment calibration  certificates  prior to  testing by the  Customer.  In addition,  prior to commencement of post­ construction  testing,  the  construction  and  maintenance  Contractor  shall  provide  the  Customer  with preliminary Operations and maintenance manuals for the Works or  site.  If any error or defect in the preliminary operation  and  maintenance  manuals  is  discovered  during  the  completion  tests,  the Contractor shall immediately correct the error or defect at his own expense.
Any product produced, as well as any income or other benefit received as a result of the launch, are the property of the Customer. Once the Work has passed each stage of the commissioning process, the Contractor shall submit to the  Customer a certified report of the  results of these tests. The report must contain the following information:

- tested material/installation or part of  the Works;
- location of the material/plant or part of the Works;
- place of  testing;
- date and time of testing;
- technical personnel supervising or conducting tests;
- equipment used and test method;
- calibration certificates for used test equipment;
- readings and measurements taken during the tests;
- test results, including any calculations and graphs;
- established acceptance criteria;
- other details specified in the Contract.
The Customer shall review each such report and may send a written notice to the Contractor if the test results do not comply with the terms of the Contract.

